e  actually knew what he was saying yeah  he's fantastic in the show i i was  really blown away by his ability with  japanese and um  he did a great job um i so i love  learning about like the behind the  scenes of the making of shows so for  both of you um what do you actually  think would surprise soon to be fans  of uh this series about the making of  the show  well i think that the one one thing is  that uh and i think you can tell is that  we  we went against everybody's conventional  wisdom and we made the entire show in  and around tokyo we shot 100 of it we  didn't build sets in another country we  didn't try to cheat vancouver or toronto  for tokyo and so i think when you're  watching there's i've had a couple  people say to me that it just feels so  authentic you feel every second of it  that you're in the city and that's by  design  yeah i think that i think what people  will be  uh hopefully surprised from tree by is  that you know this is built on the genre  of a crime show it is it's got sex it's  got violence got its mystery um  eroticism but it's character driven is  everything i try to do i make i try to  do and it's as i know allen is the same  and so  i think you'll i think people will be  pleasantly excited and surprised that  they're as they watch the episodes they  drawn in in some ways become almost many  experts in these worlds within worlds of  late 90s tokyo what is it like to  actually be a detective what is it like  to not just in a stylized way but in a  real way be a young yakuza japanese  gangster foot soldier struggling with is  this the life i want what does it say  about me and we begin to care what is  set up in the first episode to be we  think perhaps a show about this young  white westerner going through tokyo we  realized quickly as the show expands and  expands is actually just one of a larger  ensemble where in fact the majority of  the actors and stories we're following  are of japanese characters  when you were making a show about the  yakuza um  what is it actually like behind the  scenes in terms of uh are do you feel  like any do you know what i mean because  this is not an organization that likes  the limelight but many stories have been  told about the yakuza well i think what  we're doing i mean what do you think al  i think what we're doing is you know one  of the things i became fascinated wanted  to show in the writing of the scripts  and the directors into allen who you  know directed our finale being one of  them  really visually brought to life is you  know when you're behind the scenes it is  dangerous and sexy but also like you  gotta chop the onions everyone wears the  same strange by western standard strange  white jumpsuits and there's things that  are i mean this is a show that's funny  this is a show that's funny and  surprising and odd and wonderful ways as  well because  if you really get into any subculture  and explore it there's just wonderful  contradictions that come to the surface  i was going to say you know  we're we're not allowed to talk to real  yakuza but we had a number of ex yakuza  people who had successfully extricated  themselves from from the crime  syndicates who were with us as  consultants but the truth is there's a  huge genre of yakuza movies inside and  outside of japan but most mostly what  they do is they tend to glorify they  tend to look at this cool dangerous life  and instead it was important enough  important to us to say as jt says you  have to chop the onions the day-to-day  life is not so glamorous and that i  think you feel much closer to what  sato's life feels like because you're  able to see that it isn't all like  danger and fancy clothes and fast cars  that you're in a jumpsuit chopping  onions  uh i'm i i have more questions but i've  been given the wrap up side i'm just  gonna sorry i really it's all good i  want to say sincerely congrats i really  can't wait to watch the rest of the  episodes all right thank you steve  thanks so much

